# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in the values. `.env.local` is git-ignored.
#
# Next.js exposes a variable to browser code only if it is prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, and it is
# inlined at build time rather than read at run time. So these are neither secrets nor live
# configuration: whatever is here when the bundle is built is what ships in it.
#
# The engine's own configuration is `../.env`, a separate file read by a separate process. Being
# in one repository does not merge them.
#
# Full reference: ../docs/usage.md#ui

# The engine's LangGraph Server. Unset, the app runs entirely on the mock fixtures in
# `lib/mock/fixtures.ts` -- which renders a complete, plausible UI against no engine at all. That
# is useful for layout work and is the trap when you meant to be looking at live data.
NEXT_PUBLIC_LANGGRAPH_URL=http://localhost:2024

# The graph id, as declared in `../langgraph.json`.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ASSISTANT_ID=serve

# There is no credential to configure: the engine reads none off the wire.

# ── read but not `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, so browser code never sees either ──────────────────────────

# Build output directory (`next.config.ts`). Set it to run a SECOND dev server against this same
# source tree: two `next dev` processes sharing `.next` contend over it and the second dies on
# startup. Three `../.claude/launch.json` entries set it: `ui-verify`, `ui-mock-3200`,
# `ui-live-3100`.
#
#     NEXT_DIST_DIR=.next-verify npm run dev -- --port 3100
#
# Defaults to `.next`.
# NEXT_DIST_DIR=.next-verify

# Where `npm run check:api` points, and *only* that script -- it runs in Node, not in the
# browser, so the unprefixed name is deliberate and it is not inlined into any bundle. Defaults
# to http://127.0.0.1:2024. Note this is a separate variable from NEXT_PUBLIC_LANGGRAPH_URL
# above: setting one does not set the other.
# LANGGRAPH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:2024
